From 64e26a46d824ff18ed59cfd0af4a322268aacc9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yasoteien <263659156+yasoteien@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 16:07:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: rk8xx: Also set DEV_OFF in the power-off prepare step

Since "mfd: rk8xx: Register poweroff handler in atomic phase" (6.18.65),
rk808_power_off() runs only in the final power-off step, after
smp_send_stop() and with interrupts off. The I2C transfer there uses the
polling path of i2c-rk3x, but the regmap lock is a mutex: if another CPU
was stopped while it held the lock (the RK818 charger monitor reads the
PMIC every few seconds and has no .shutdown), the write cannot be done
and the board is left to the watchdog, or stays on.

Register rk808_power_off() for the prepare step as well, as before
6.18.65. There the other CPUs still run, the lock is released and the
transfer may sleep; this is the path that switched the Pomera DM250 off
in every test until 6.18.64. The write in the final step stays as a
fallback for boards where the prepare step cannot reach the PMIC (the
Veyron case of the upstream change). The wait handler added by
"Register a final power-off handler so that poweroff does not halt"
still runs last, since rk808_power_off() returns right after the write.

Also update the comments that described the old order.

Signed-off-by: yasoteien <263659156+yasoteien@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c             | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++-------
 drivers/power/supply/rk818_charger.c |  9 ++++----
 2 files changed, 28 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c b/drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c
index 3e955b5c9..f32bd7dab 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c
@@ -656,9 +656,10 @@ static int rk808_power_off(struct sys_off_data *data)
 }
 
 /*
- * The supplies are switched off in the prepare step, where the I2C bus may
- * still sleep. This handler only waits for that to take effect, so that
- * machine_power_off() does not return while the PMIC is still on.
+ * rk808_power_off() sets DEV_OFF and returns at once. This handler runs
+ * after it in the final power-off step and only waits for the supplies to
+ * drop, so that machine_power_off() does not return while the PMIC is
+ * still on.
  */
 #define RK8XX_POWER_OFF_WAIT_MS		5000
 
@@ -837,10 +838,26 @@ int rk8xx_probe(struct device *dev, int variant, unsigned int irq, struct regmap
 					     "failed to register poweroff handler\n");
 
 		/*
-		 * Without a handler for the final power-off step,
-		 * kernel_can_power_off() is false when nothing else (PSCI,
-		 * gpio-poweroff, ...) can power off, and reboot(POWER_OFF)
-		 * is turned into a halt with the PMIC still on.
+		 * Also set DEV_OFF in the prepare step, where the other CPUs
+		 * still run and the I2C transfer may sleep. In the final step
+		 * the other CPUs are stopped; if one was stopped while holding
+		 * the regmap lock (the charger monitor reads the PMIC every
+		 * few seconds), the write there cannot be done. The write in
+		 * the final step stays as a fallback for boards where the
+		 * prepare step cannot reach the PMIC.
+		 */
+		ret = devm_register_sys_off_handler(dev,
+				    SYS_OFF_MODE_POWER_OFF_PREPARE, SYS_OFF_PRIO_HIGH,
+				    &rk808_power_off, rk808);
+		if (ret)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+					     "failed to register poweroff prepare handler\n");
+
+		/*
+		 * rk808_power_off() returns right after the I2C write. Wait
+		 * here, or reboot(POWER_OFF) goes on to do_exit() and the
+		 * hw_protection fallback to emergency_restart() while the
+		 * PMIC is still switching off.
 		 */
 		ret = devm_register_sys_off_handler(dev,
 				    SYS_OFF_MODE_POWER_OFF, SYS_OFF_PRIO_LOW,
diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/rk818_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/rk818_charger.c
index 4790a8a79..7d200c230 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/rk818_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/rk818_charger.c
@@ -1242,11 +1242,10 @@ static void rk818_monitor_work(struct work_struct *work)
 
 /*
  * The forced step of the low battery power-off. The kernel's own one
- * (hw_failure_emergency_action_func) calls kernel_power_off(), which always
- * returns on this board: the RK818 is switched off in the power-off prepare
- * step and nothing handles the final step. It then restarts the system
- * at once. Here DEV_OFF is set directly instead, unless the battery is no
- * longer low by the same test that started the power-off.
+ * (hw_failure_emergency_action_func) calls kernel_power_off(), and if that
+ * returns it restarts the system at once, which can win against the
+ * PMIC switching off. Here DEV_OFF is set directly instead, unless the
+ * battery is no longer low by the same test that started the power-off.
  */
 static void rk818_off_work(struct work_struct *work)
 {
-- 
